    By default, ``jnp.nanmax`` computes the maximum of elements along the flattened
    array.

    >>> nan = jnp.nan
    >>> x = jnp.array([[8, nan, 4, 6],
    ...                [nan, -2, nan, -4],
    ...                [-2, 1, 7, nan]])
    >>> jnp.nanmax(x)
    Array(8., dtype=float32)

    If ``axis=1``, the maximum will be computed along axis 1.

